<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brahmeswara Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1058</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Brahmeshvara Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Brahmeswara Temple is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhubaneswar">Bhubaneswar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, built around 1058 according to an inscription that credits Queen Kolavati, mother of the Somavamshi king Udyota Keshari. Set in a courtyard with four smaller shrines at its corners, it is a mature example of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> style of temple architecture, and its walls carry richly carved figures, musicians and dancers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brahmeswara-temple.jpg" alt="Brahmeswara Temple" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Udayakuni · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brahmeswar_Temple,_Bhubaneswar.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
